    "description": "Health data analytics turns measurements and records into evidence about population health and healthcare performance. Iran’s risk-factor surveillance provides a concrete example of how sampling, laboratory measurements, and explicit indicator definitions support analysis while limiting what can be inferred.",
    "tags": ["Health statistics", "Population health", "STEPS", "Data quality", "Epidemiology"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T05:13:23Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T05:13:23Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Health Data Analytics in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Iranian health analytics through risk-factor surveillance, diabetes care analysis, and the challenges of comparing health data."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "analytics-purpose",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From measurements to interpretable evidence",
        "content": "Health data become useful when an analysis connects a clearly defined question to an appropriate population and measurement method. The World Health Organization’s STEPwise approach to noncommunicable disease risk-factor surveillance, known as STEPS, provides a standardized framework for collecting, analysing, and communicating such information. Noncommunicable diseases are conditions such as diabetes and cardiovascular disease that are not transmitted between people. [s1]\n\nSTEPS covers behavioural exposures, including tobacco use, diet, and physical activity, alongside biological risk factors such as raised blood pressure and glucose. Its purpose is to make information useful for public health decisions and comparisons. Standard questions and protocols support consistency, but comparisons still require attention to the people included and the indicator being calculated. [s1]\n\nFor understanding Iran, this offers a practical entry point into analytics: the important technology is not only a dashboard. It is also the survey instrument, sampling design, measurement protocol, and definition that determine what a displayed result actually means. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "iranian-example",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Using surveillance to examine diabetes care",
        "content": "An analysis of Iran’s 2021 STEPS survey examined diabetes prevalence and the care cascade—the progression from having a condition to knowing about it, receiving treatment, and achieving a defined control measure. The underlying survey combined questionnaires, physical measurements, and laboratory testing, with recruitment covering urban and rural populations across Iran. The diabetes analysis applied its own age restrictions and explicit disease definitions. [s2]\n\nThis illustrates why denominators matter. Treatment coverage among everyone classified as having diabetes answers a different question from glucose control among people already diagnosed. Changing the denominator can change the percentage without any contradiction. Similarly, an indicator based on reported medication use is different from one established by laboratory measurement. [s2]\n\nThe researchers also compared selected measures with WHO coverage targets. Such comparisons depend on matching the target population and control definition. The analysis therefore demonstrates both the usefulness of national surveillance and the care needed when turning it into an assessment of service performance. A survey association or gap is evidence for investigation; it does not by itself identify a cause. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "historical-comparability",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why older data need their original context",
        "content": "Iran’s risk-factor surveillance has an earlier documentary record. The national data book for 2007 describes a population-based survey and presents results by demographic and geographic group. Its stated age coverage differs from that used in the later diabetes analysis. This is a concrete reason to retain the scope of each source rather than attaching successive publication dates to a seemingly uniform trend. [s3][s2]\n\nThe older report also makes the distinction between questionnaire responses, physical measurements, and biochemical testing visible. These forms of evidence answer related but different questions. An account of reported behaviour cannot simply substitute for a measured clinical indicator, even when both concern the same health risk. [s3]\n\nFor readers, the most useful habit is to ask what was measured, among whom, and under which definition before interpreting a chart. Iran’s surveillance publications provide substantive analytical material, but their existence does not establish a continuous annual dataset. Preserving methodological differences makes the evidence more informative and prevents apparent precision from concealing changes in coverage. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "survey-to-analysis",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "How the diabetes analysis used surveillance evidence",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"Iran STEPS survey sample\"] -->|\"Collect responses\"| B[\"Questionnaires\"]\n  A -->|\"Measure participants\"| C[\"Physical measurements\"]\n  A -->|\"Test eligible participants\"| D[\"Laboratory assessments\"]\n  B -->|\"Apply study definitions\"| E[\"Diabetes care indicators\"]\n  C -->|\"Apply study definitions\"| E\n  D -->|\"Apply study definitions\"| E\n  E -->|\"Match eligible groups and thresholds\"| F[\"Compare selected WHO targets\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The diagram summarizes the study’s analytical inputs. Arrows indicate collection and analysis steps, not a live national data exchange system.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      }
    ],
